  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonally warm temperatures and comfortable \nhumidity continue through Wednesday.\n\nAn upper trough is located across the Mid-Atlantic and \nNortheast early this morning. At the surface, a weak cold front \nis pushing S across the region. Mostly clear and pleasant with \ntemperatures primarily in the 60s. Surface high pressure over \nthe Midwest will build across the Mid-Atlantic in the wake of \nthe cold front today through tonight, before settling offshore \nWednesday. Comfortable humidity levels for later August will \ncontinue today with high temperatures in the lower to mid 80s. \nMostly clear tonight with low temperatures ranging from the \nupper 50s NW to upper 60s SE. High temperatures increase a few \ndegrees into the mid/upper 80s Wednesday as high pressure \nsettles offshore. Dewpoints will increase as well, but overall, \nhumidity levels will remain tolerable for August.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled conditions return mid to late week \nwith daily chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nGlobal models indicate that surface high pressure settles off \nthe Southeast coast later Wednesday into Thursday and the flow \naloft shifts back to the SW. A few showers/tstms could creep \ninto western portions of the area Wednesday evening. Otherwise, \nThursday and Friday have the best potential for more coverage of\nshowers/tstms as a slow moving front slowly approaches the \nregion. The severe risk appears to be rather low both days with \n500mb flow around 20-25kt and the potential for morning clouds \nand a few showers both days. However, if precip can hold off \nuntil the afternoon hours a few stronger tstms are possible, \nespecially given that our local area will be under the RRQ of a \nstrengthening jet core over New England. The main threat will be\nlocally heavy rain from showers/tstms. However, this threat is \nnot particularly significant at this time given that there is \nsome mid-level flow, and uncertainty with regard to organization\nand overall coverage. The ERO is only 'Marginal' both Thursday \nand Friday. The consensus amongst the 00z/25 models pushes the \nfront S Saturday with the best shower/tstm chances across SE \nVA/NE NC, then trending drier Sunday/Monday with PoPs near to \nslightly below climo.\n\nSeasonally hot and humid Thursday through Sunday with high \ntemperatures primarily in the mid to upper 80s, and potentially \ntrending above average into the upper 80s to lower 90s by \nMonday.",
